  "observations": [
    "Every data sheet checked is divided into blocks. None is the single flat table the code assumed.",
    "The jurisdictions present are ACT, NSW, QLD, SA and TAS. Victoria is absent throughout, which is correct: Victorian retail is reported to the Essential Services Commission, not the AER.",
    "A 'Jurisdictions' row is a section divider, not a jurisdiction. So is 'Tier 1 Retailers'.",
    "'Tier 1 Retailers' introduces a second pass over the same jurisdictions for a subset of retailers. That is a dimension the schema does not have, and without it the Tier 1 National block and the all-retailers National block share a key.",
    "The sheet-level cell A4 names only the FIRST block's jurisdiction. Read as the whole sheet's jurisdiction it mislabels every later block.",
    "Widest columns run to BN, BX and CC. A fixture capped at column 40 (AN) cannot see the annual column block, which is why six sheets were refused outright on the first ingest.",
    "A dimension can also be stated across column groups, not just per row or per sheet. 'Switch# Standard to Market' has fuel on row 3 (B3=Electricity, AJ3=Gas) and customer segment on row 4 (B4-K4=Residential, M4-V4=Small Business, X4-AG4=Large), the measure on row 5 and the quarter on row 6.",
    "The AER mixes two spellings of a group label inside one sheet: row 4 repeats its label in every column, while row 3 writes it once and leaves the rest of the group empty. So a column's group value is the nearest label at or to its left, not a direct read.",
    "21 of Schedule 3's 29 sheets have at least one header row whose labels are written once per column group. Measured by reading the workbook, not inferred: for that schedule this is the dominant shape rather than an edge case.",
    "'Switching' is transposed - rows are quarters, columns are jurisdictions, and there is no retailer column at all - so it cannot be represented by this schema and is skipped by name."
  ]
